{-
   The first declarations the compiler *implements* - `compiler/intrinsic/op.def`.

   # resolver: ownership

   `@builtin(role)` on a signature with no body says that the compiler supplies what it does. The
   signature is still the signature: `readIt` below is checked against it, its argument is prepared
   under the declared contract, and what the role decides is only which node the call site builds at
   the end - see `resolve_mir/intrinsic/expand.h`.

   The declarations are `Native`'s, and this fixture does not write them: `prelude/Native/Pointer.yana`
   does, and an ordinary `import` is what reaches them. That is the arrangement `lib/Native` will be
   in - the prelude is a library, found by the same walk and bound by the same attribute - so what is
   here is a program that *calls* the family.

   The load is spelled `load` and the real library spells it `*`, which is not sloppiness - it is what
   the attribute buys. A role is bound by `@builtin`, so the compiler holds no name for any of these
   and the library is free to call them whatever reads best. The prefix-operator *syntax* is a
   separate thing this stage does not resolve yet, which is also why the arithmetic is `advance` and
   `retreat` here and `+` and `-` there.

   `store`'s second parameter is `->value` and the shape says so, which is the whole reason the check
   exists: the pointee holds the value afterwards, so the frame that owned it stops owing it a
   teardown. A declaration that wrote a plain parameter there is refused at the declaration - see
   `Reject.Shape.yana` beside this.

   **What the answers are for.** Every function below is reached from `main` and the sum is what
   `.run.expect` holds, because none of this is visible in the emitted text: a stride computed as a
   size rather than as a stride, a `borrow` of the wrong address, or a `store` through a copy all
   produce well-formed IR and a different number.
-}

import Native

-- Reading and writing what a pointer names. Both are *places* rather than operations - the MIR has
-- an address root, so the load is a `read` of it and the store is a `write`, and nothing about
-- ownership, loans or drop elaboration needs a case for either.
fn readIt(p: %I32) -> I32 = load(p)

fn writeIt(p: %I32, ->v: I32) -> {} = store(p, v)

{-
   `addressOf`, and the qualifier that makes it more than a cast.

   The declaration is `fn addressOf(it: source'a) -> %source'a`: the parameter publishes an origin and
   the result names it, so the storage `it` names stays live to the pointer's last use and this
   signature can say the same thing one level up. Written `it: a` the argument would be *copied* at
   every scalar and the address would be the copy's - the wrong storage, and rooted in nothing.
-}
fn through(r: source'I32) -> %source'I32 = addressOf(r)

fn roundTrip() -> Int:
    let &v = 41 :: I32
    let p = through(v)
    store(p, load(p) + 1)

    -- Read back through the *name* rather than through the pointer, so that a `store` which wrote a
    -- temporary would be visible here as 41.
    return v

{-
   `borrow` and `borrowMut` - the one place the checked world is re-entered from the unchecked one.

   Nothing verified that the pointer is valid or that what it names is initialized; what the
   signature does promise is where the result is rooted, which is what gives the borrow the caller's
   extent instead of the call's. The two differ in the access axis and in nothing else, which is why
   `op.def` has two shape fields rather than one enum of the combinations.
-}
fn viaBorrow(r: source'I32) -> Int:
    -- Bound rather than returned directly: the borrow is a loan of the caller's storage and the
    -- result is an owned `Int`, so what crosses the two is the ordinary copy a `let` of a trivially
    -- copyable value makes.
    let seen = borrow(addressOf(r))
    return seen + 0

fn viaBorrowMut(&r: I32) -> Int:
    let &seen = borrowMut(addressOf(r))
    seen = seen * 2
    return r

{-
   Arithmetic in **elements**, and the record is the case that makes it worth testing.

   `Pad` is nine bytes and sixteen apart: its trailing padding belongs to whoever contains it, so
   `size` is what a whole-value copy moves and `stride` is what the next element starts at. Code that
   walks storage by the size overlaps its own elements, and the two numbers differ for exactly this
   shape - which is why `stride` is the measurement the compiler emits and `size` is not.
-}
data Pad {wide: I64, narrow: I8}

fn walked() -> Int:
    let &pads = [Pad {wide: 1, narrow: 2}, Pad {wide: 3, narrow: 4}] :: [Pad *2]

    let base = addressOf(pads[0])
    let next = advance(base, 1)

    -- Three answers from one pair of addresses: what the second element holds, that stepping back
    -- lands on the first again, and how many elements apart the two are.
    let stepped = load(next).wide
    let back = load(retreat(next, 1)).wide

    return truncate(stepped * 10 + back + difference(base, next))

{-
   The stride at a type the body **does not know** - the half that is not a constant.

   `@generic` rather than measured, because what is being asserted is the second lowering and not the
   heuristic: an erased body reads its metrics out of the descriptor its caller supplied, so the
   scale here is a load, a mask, a shift and a rounding rather than an immediate. That is the whole
   reason a stride is a node in the IR instead of a number the conversion folded - a body compiled
   once has no layout to fold.

   Called at two types whose strides differ, which is what makes the answer say the descriptor was
   read rather than a constant baked in: one `secondOf` in the image, two answers.
-}
@generic fn secondOf(xs: &[a *2]) -> a:
    let base = addressOf(xs[0])
    return load(advance(base, 1))

fn erased() -> Int:
    let &ns = [3, 9] :: [I32 *2]
    let &pads = [Pad {wide: 1, narrow: 2}, Pad {wide: 4, narrow: 5}] :: [Pad *2]

    return secondOf(ns) + truncate(secondOf(pads).wide)

fn main() -> Int:
    let &n = 7 :: I32

    writeIt(addressOf(n), 5)
    let read = readIt(addressOf(n))

    return read + roundTrip() + viaBorrow(n) + viaBorrowMut(n) + walked() + erased()
